---
import LanguageServerDoc from "@components/docs/LanguageServerDoc.astro";
import DocsLayout from "@layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Python" description="Python language-server support in Axon">
  <LanguageServerDoc
    title="Python"
    description="Axon uses Pyright for Python language intelligence. Pyright provides completions, diagnostics, hover, definitions, references, symbols, rename, import analysis, and type-aware navigation for Python workspaces."
    server="pyright-langserver"
    delivery="bundled npm server"
    activation="Starts for .py files and Python workspaces; interpreter selection is shown when Python files are present"
    overview={[
      "Python is sensitive to the interpreter used by the project. A file can be valid Python, but imports such as Django, DRF, FastAPI, NumPy, Celery, or a local app package will not resolve unless the server analyzes the environment where those packages are installed.",
      "Axon should work for basic Python syntax without forcing a virtual environment selection. The interpreter picker becomes important when imports live outside the system Python or when the project uses a venv, Poetry, Pipenv, pyenv, or another environment manager.",
      "The editor should only surface Python environment controls when the workspace actually contains Python. Showing Python loading or failed states in a non-Python workspace creates noise and makes LSP status feel broken even when the server is simply irrelevant.",
    ]}
    capabilities={[
      "Completions for modules, classes, functions, methods, local variables, typed attributes, imported names, and project-local packages.",
      "Hover for inferred types, function signatures, docstrings, imported symbols, classes, modules, and variables.",
      "Diagnostics for missing imports, type issues, syntax errors, unreachable code, invalid calls, and project configuration problems.",
      "Go to definition, references, rename, document symbols, workspace symbols, and import-aware navigation through the selected environment.",
    ]}
    setup={[
      {
        title: "Select the project interpreter when imports are external",
        body: "Use the interpreter or virtual environment that the project itself uses. For a Django REST Framework project, that is usually the venv where django, djangorestframework, and the project's other dependencies are installed.",
      },
      {
        title: "Open the backend or project root",
        body: "Open the folder that owns pyproject.toml, requirements.txt, manage.py, setup.py, poetry.lock, or the source package. Opening the wrong parent or a nested folder can prevent Pyright from seeing project-local imports.",
      },
      {
        title: "Do not confuse syntax support with environment support",
        body: "Basic completions can work without a selected interpreter, but accurate third-party imports need the environment. This is the same reason larger editors ask for or auto-detect a Python interpreter.",
      },
    ]}
    packaging={[
      "Pyright is bundled as an npm server, so official Axon releases should not require a global pyright install.",
      "The selected interpreter path points to the user's local Python or virtual environment. Axon does not bundle the user's project packages; it tells Pyright which Python environment should be used for analysis.",
    ]}
    troubleshooting={[
      {
        title: "The server exits after selecting a venv",
        body: "Check that the interpreter path still exists, is executable, and belongs to the same architecture as the app environment. Also check LSP logs for Pyright startup arguments and path errors.",
      },
      {
        title: "Imports are still unresolved",
        body: "Confirm the selected interpreter has the package installed, the workspace root is correct, and project-local source folders are visible from the opened folder.",
      },
      {
        title: "Python appears in non-Python projects",
        body: "That is an Axon UX bug. Python status and environment controls should be scoped to workspaces or files where Python is relevant.",
      },
    ]}
  />
</DocsLayout>
